  "interpretation": "At 4,000 RPS, pure BAML automatically collected throughout the five-minute run. The 73 sampled resets occurred about every four seconds, and each minute retained nearly the same malloc-live sawtooth: median live bytes moved only from 58.07 MiB in minute one to 61.14 MiB in minute five. RSS rose in discrete steps with malloc-reserved capacity, including a later expansion between roughly 154 and 204 seconds, and reached 184.25 MiB while reserved capacity remained exactly 184 MiB for the final 111 seconds. One final explicit collection returned the managed runtime to 15 objects, weak permit registrations to five, and malloc-live memory to 19.18 MiB without reducing RSS or reserved capacity. This rules out a request-proportional BAML-managed live-object leak over the measured interval, but it does not rule out a process-memory leak. The 148 MiB increase in allocator-reserved capacity despite a stable live-memory envelope is suggestive of allocator retention or fragmentation caused by the workload. Because capacity expanded again after more than a minute of apparent stability, the final 90-second flat segment is insufficient to establish a permanent plateau or distinguish a bounded allocator high-water mark from slower unbounded retention."
